# CODEOWNERS — review routing for the security-sensitive config surfaces.
#
# The load-bearing line is the grants overlay: the access-request flow's bot-PRs edit
# ONLY agent/access/grants.generated.ts, and with hundreds of people able to READ the
# config repo, this line + branch protection ("require review from Code Owners") is what makes
# GitHub's merge button the flow's real approval gate — only the owners below can put a
# grant live. See docs/how-it-works/access-requests.md (runbook: branch protection must
# be enabled by a repo admin; CODEOWNERS alone doesn't block a merge).
#
# All three overlay lines below name a `*.generated.ts` file (OPE-256): the overlays are
# machine-owned DATA MODULES, not JSON. That change moved nothing about who gates them — it
# is `npm run lint:overlays`, a leg of the deterministic test chain, that proves such a file
# still contains only data and no executable code. This repo runs no GitHub Actions workflow,
# so nothing here is a required check; the chain runs in the session that makes the change.
#
# Operators forking this repo: replace the owner with your own admin team/users.
#
# ── BASELINE: EVERYTHING IS OWNED ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# GitHub CODEOWNERS is LAST-MATCH-WINS, so this catch-all must stay FIRST — every specific line
# below overrides it for its own path. It exists because the question "why would anything not be
# protected?" has no good answer: an unowned path is one a PR can change with no required review,
# and the interesting attack is never on the file you remembered to list. The specific lines are
# kept BELOW it and are not redundant — they are the record of WHICH paths are security-load-bearing
# and WHY, which a catch-all cannot express. Keep both.
#
# Note the catch-all deliberately does NOT satisfy `test:codeowners`: that test asserts every
# bot-PR target is SPECIFICALLY owned, and ignores this line when doing so. Otherwise adding a
# catch-all would make the whole invariant vacuously true — the check would pass for a path nobody
# had ever thought about, which is the opposite of what it is for.
* @open-everywhere/maintainers

/agent/access/grants.generated.ts @open-everywhere/maintainers

