# Role definitions — `applyRoleDefaults` fills in two of a domain's fields when the domain omits
# them (roles/role.ts: `memory`, `sandbox`; the domain's own value always wins). So re-pointing a
# role changes the effective configuration of every domain that leans on those defaults, WITHOUT
# touching any path protected above. That is what this line is for.
#
# Both fields are live and both are consequential:
#   • `memory` IS read (memory/domain.ts silo list → memory/resolve.ts) — a role re-point changes
#     WHICH BRAIN a domain reads and writes, which is the memory-sharing boundary itself; and
#   • `sandbox` IS read (roles/role.ts sandboxPresetFor) — it changes the execution preset.
#
# What a role can NOT do is grant capability. Neither a Role nor a Domain has an access field at
# all, so the only way to widen what a domain reaches is a binding in
# agent/access/bundles.ts — codeowned above, and the sole list resolveAccess reads. Likewise a role
# cannot supply `admins` or `governance`: applyRoleDefaults passes only those two fields, so the
# RBAC roster stays sole-sourced from the codeowned domains.ts above.
/agent/roles/ @open-everywhere/maintainers
